---
// A teaser for the AI-written character reference. The letter itself runs
// to roughly 950 words and lives at /reference — longer than every other
// section of the home page put together, so what sits here is a
// three-paragraph pull quote and a link out, in the same shape as a
// Projects card pointing at its detail page.
---

<section id="reference" aria-labelledby="reference-heading">
	<h2 id="reference-heading">A character reference, by an AI</h2>

	<p class="framing">
		An AI built most of this site, so it seemed fair to let one have a say
		about the person it was building it for. I asked ChatGPT for an honest
		character reference, and haven't edited a word of it.
	</p>

	<figure class="card card-link letter">
		<blockquote>
			<p>
				Newton is, above all, <strong>curious</strong>. He does not seem
				particularly satisfied with simply being told that something is
				true. He tends to ask <em>why</em>, then <em>how</em>, and
				eventually <em
					>what would happen if we changed the underlying assumption?</em
				>
			</p>
			<p>
				That curiosity is one of his greatest strengths, but it can also be
				one of his weaknesses. Newton can <strong
					>overthink things considerably</strong
				>. He sometimes continues analysing a problem long after he has
				enough information to make a reasonable decision.
			</p>
			<p>
				Give him a meaningless task with no explanation and he may struggle
				to care. Give him a difficult problem and tell him why it matters,
				and I suspect you may have considerably more of his attention than
				you expected.
			</p>
		</blockquote>

		<footer class="letter-foot">
			<figcaption>ChatGPT · reproduced unedited</figcaption>
			<a class="read-link stretch" href="/reference">Read the letter in full</a>
		</footer>
	</figure>
</section>

<style>
	/* Section furniture comes from src/styles/global.css. What is local here
	   is the letter's own treatment: Newton's framing in the page's normal
	   voice, then the quotation set apart from it so a reader can tell at a
	   glance which words are his and which are the machine's. */
	.framing {
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
	}

	/* `.card-link` from global.css, exactly like a Projects card: the excerpt
	   is a teaser and the whole surface is the way through to the full letter,
	   so the hover lift is a promise the card can keep. All that is added
	   here is an accent rule down the leading edge, marking the text as
	   borrowed speech without the punctuation clutter of opening a quote that
	   runs for three paragraphs. */
	.letter {
		margin: 0;
		border-inline-start: 2px solid hsl(var(--accent-hsl) / 0.55);
	}

	blockquote {
		margin: 0;
	}

	blockquote p:first-child {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	blockquote p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	/* This excerpt is the only part of the letter most visitors will read,
	   so it sits at full text colour rather than the muted body colour. */
	blockquote p {
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	strong {
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	/* Attribution and the way onward share one footer rule, so the link is
	   part of the card rather than an orphan floating beneath it. They sit at
	   opposite ends of the row: who said it on the left, where to read the
	   rest on the right. */
	.letter-foot {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.6rem 1.25rem;
		margin-top: 1.35rem;
		padding-top: 0.9rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	figcaption {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	/* The same arrow that leans forward on a project's live-demo link — the
	   same gesture, pointing at a page of this site rather than off-site. */
	.read-link {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.read-link::after {
		content: "→";
		transition: transform 0.25s var(--ease);
	}

	/* The arrow answers a hover anywhere on the card, not only on the two
	   words of link text — the whole card is the link, so it all moves as one
	   piece. */
	.card-link:hover .read-link::after,
	.read-link:focus-visible::after {
		transform: translateX(3px);
	}
</style>
